    "https://external.jobs.uwa.edu.au/jobs/senior-plumbing-officer-crawley-wa-australia": {
      "title": "Senior Plumbing Officer",
      "description": "Senior Plumbing Officer - Crawley, WA, Australia Back to UWA My Profile Kiosk mode Pause the proceeding carousel Senior Plumbing Officer 524495 Crawley, WA Full Time Closing on: 24 Nov 2026 Primarily a hands-on plumbing role undertaking preventative and reactive maintenance. Varied fault-finding, repair, installation and minor works across a diverse campus environment. Full-time appointment on an ongoing basis. Base salary range: $108,413 – $120,287 p.a. (pro-rata) plus 17% superannuation. About the area Campus Management is responsible for the planning, development, management and operation of the University's physical estate and infrastructure. The portfolio delivers integrated property, facilities, asset management, capital works and operational services that support the University's strategic objectives by providing safe, sustainable and efficient environments for teaching, research and campus life. Through responsible stewardship of the University's built, natural and cultural assets, Campus Management protects heritage values, recognises Indigenous connections to Country, and supports inclusive, sustainable and future-focused campus development. We are seeking an experienced and capable plumber to join our Campus Maintenance team in a predominantly hands-on role supporting the University’s extensive plumbing infrastructure. Reporting to the Plumbing Team Lead as one of the Senior Plumbing Officers, you will undertake a broad range of preventative and reactive maintenance activities across campus, including fault finding, repairs, installations, inspections and minor works. No two days are the same, with work spanning laboratories, teaching facilities, student accommodation, heritage buildings and critical infrastructure. About the opportunity Using your trade expertise, you will diagnose and resolve complex plumbing issues, provide technical advice when required, and contribute to the safe, reliable and compliant operation of the University’s plumbing assets. This is not a dedicated supervisory or office-based position, and you will work within a team of other Plumbers and Senior Plumbing Officers. While the role will occasionally assist with the oversight of contractors and provide technical guidance on specialised works, its primary focus is the hands-on delivery of plumbing maintenance and repairs across the University. Perform preventative and reactive plumbing maintenance across UWA facilities and infrastructure, and diagnose faults and undertake repairs to plumbing, drainage, gas and associated systems. Carry out inspections, testing and maintenance activities in accordance with relevant regulations and standards, while interpreting plans and drawings to support maintenance, installation and repair work. Provide practical technical advice and recommendations relating to plumbing systems and infrastructure, and assist with minor project works, asset inspections and condition assessments. Contribute to a strong safety culture and ensure compliance with relevant legislative and University requirements, and support the engagement and monitoring of contractors undertaking specialist plumbing works when required. About you You are an experienced plumber who enjoys practical problem solving, takes pride in delivering quality workmanship and is comfortable working independently across a wide variety of maintenance environments. You will have: Trade or technical plumbing qualifications together with a Plumbing license and Gas license type ‘G’. Extensive experience delivering preventative and reactive plumbing maintenance, repairs and installations. Strong troubleshooting and fault-finding capability across a range of plumbing systems. Sound knowledge of current plumbing regulations, standards and industry best practice. Well-developed organisational and communication skills. The ability to work autonomously while contributing positively as part of a team. Experience in the maintenance or restoration of heritage buildings would be highly regarded . Note : As part of the recruitment process, candidates will be required to complete declarations in accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence. A current National Police Clearance, “C” class driver’s licence, Senior First Aid Certificate and High-Risk Work Licence will be required by the successful applicant. Gas Servicing Licence and Backflow Licence are highly desirable. This role may also require participation in an on-call roster and occasional weekend work. Position description: PD - Senior Plumbing Officer (524495).pdf To learn more about this opportunity, please contact Melita O’Donnell at melita.odonnell@uwa.edu.au For enquiries on the recruitment process, please contact Shelby Corrins at shelby.corrins@uwa.edu.au How to apply Please apply online via the Apply Now button. The content of your Resume (max. 4 pages) and Cover Letter (max. 2 pages) should demonstrate how you meet the selection criteria. Closing date: 11:55 PM AWST on Tuesday, 24 November 2026 This position is only open to applicants with relevant rights to work in Australia. Please note: Unsolicited applications and emails from recruitment agencies will not be accepted outside of formal channels. Benefits Flexible working options. Generous paid leave including parental leave. Up to 17% employer paid superannuation. Salary packaging available. Incremental progression based on 12 months continuous service. Up to 25% discount on eligible UWA full-fee courses. Discounted health insurance. Employee Assistance Program. About the University The University of Western Australia (UWA) is ranked among the top 100 universities in the world and a member of the prestigious Australian Group of Eight research intensive universities. With a strong research track record, vibrant campus and working environments, supported by the freedom to innovate and inspire, there is no better time to join Western Australia’s top university. Learn more about us. Our commitment to inclusion and diversity UWA is committed to a diverse workforce and an equitable and inclusive workplace. We are committed to fostering a safe environment for all, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, women, those from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, the LGBTIQA+ community, and people living with disability. If you require any reasonable adjustments, we encourage you to advise us at the time of application. Alternatively, you can contact us for assistance during the recruitment process. If you have queries relating to your application, please contact the individual named in the advertisement. To enable a quick response, please include the 6-digit job reference number. Back to job search Other jobs Digital Coordinator Crawley, WA Community Engagement/Marketing Bring your digital expertise to UWA and help deliver engaging, effective online experiences for diverse audiences. Enterprise Engagement Consultant Crawley, WA Business Development Lead defence engagement and support veteran pathways into higher education. Lecturer Occupational Therapist Geraldton, WA Health and Medical Sciences Develop and facilitate discipline-specific, interprofessional, project-based and evaluation-focused placement opportunities for occupational therapy and other allied health students. Benefits Flexible working options considered. 26 weeks paid parental leave after one year and 36 weeks after five years continuous service, regardless of gender. 4 weeks annual leave with the option to purchase more. 13 weeks long service leave after seven/ten years. Salary packaging options. 17% superannuation, with the option to reduce to the minimum super guarantee. Up to 25% discount on eligible UWA full-fee courses. Discounted health insurance, and convenient on-campus childcare options. Incremental progression based on 12 months continuous service. Excluding casual positions. The University of Western Australia acknowledges that its campus is situated on Noongar land, and that Noongar people remain the spiritual and cultural custodians of their land, and continue to practise their values, languages, beliefs and knowledge. Indigenous commitment Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X Youtube Cookie Preferences Privacy Policy © 2026 The University of Western Australia Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
