    "https://external.jobs.uwa.edu.au/jobs/senior-lecturer-physics-gravitational-wave-instrumentation-crawley-wa-australia": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer - Physics (Gravitational Wave Instrumentation)",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer - Physics (Gravitational Wave Instrumentation) - Crawley, WA, Australia Back to UWA My Profile Kiosk mode Pause the proceeding carousel Senior Lecturer - Physics (Gravitational Wave Instrumentation) 524871 Crawley, WA Full Time Closing on: 15 Dec 2026 Join a globally recognised research community advancing the next generation of gravitational wave detectors through the ARC Centre of Excellence for Gravitational Wave Discovery (OzGrav), the LIGO Scientific Collaboration and the International Gravitational-Wave Observatory Network (IGWN). Lead research in instrumentation for gravitational wave detection, spanning precision measurement, interferometry, quantum-limited sensing and advanced detector technologies, while helping shape the future of physics education and research at UWA. Full-time appointment on a tenurable basis. Base salary range: Level C, $149,909 – $172,441 p.a. (pro-rata) plus 17% superannuation. About the area The Department of Physics is part of UWA's School of Physics, Mathematics and Computing and delivers undergraduate and postgraduate education alongside internationally recognised research in gravitational wave science, astronomy and astrophysics, precision measurement and quantum technology, photonics and terahertz science, and medical and biomedical physics. As the Western Australian node of OzGrav, the Department plays a leading role in global gravitational wave research. Our researchers are members of the LIGO Scientific Collaboration and operate the Gingin High Optical Power Facility, a unique experimental facility dedicated to advancing interferometry and high-power laser technologies for next-generation gravitational wave observatories. The Department also maintains strong partnerships with industry and research organisations in Australia and internationally. About the opportunity Deliver high-quality teaching and academic leadership across undergraduate and postgraduate Physics programs, including curriculum development, research supervision, laboratory teaching and student mentoring. Build and lead an internationally competitive research program in gravitational wave instrumentation, contributing to publications, competitive grant success, national and international collaborations, and the development of advanced detector technologies. Design, commission and operate cutting-edge experimental systems for gravitational wave detection, contributing to major research programs within OzGrav, LIGO and future third-generation gravitational wave observatories. Work at the Department's off-campus facilities, including the Gingin High Optical Power Facility, with periodic travel within Australia and overseas for collaboration meetings and work at partner detector sites, and some out-of-hours work to support experimental runs across international time zones. About you PhD in Physics or an academic field relevant to the discipline, with a strong national or international research profile demonstrated through high-quality publications and success in securing competitive research funding. Demonstrated expertise in experimental gravitational wave detector technology, with substantial research contributions to advanced detector noise reduction in one or more areas such as seismic noise, thermal noise, parametric instability control, or quantum and shot noise. Demonstrated experience developing one or more enabling technologies for third-generation or kilohertz-band gravitational wave detectors, such as low-thermal-noise resonators, white-light cavity techniques or optomechanical devices. Proven experience supervising Higher Degree by Research, Masters and Honours students, together with a strong commitment to excellence and innovation in teaching, learning and assessment. Experience in precision optical measurement, including developing, building, commissioning and characterising complex experimental apparatus and analysing research data within collaborative research environments. Demonstrated ability to build productive relationships across large-scale international collaborations and engage effectively with students, colleagues, industry, government and broader community stakeholders. Note : As part of the recruitment process, candidates will be required to complete declarations in accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence. Position description: PD [Senior Lecturer - Physics (Gravitational Wave Instrumentation)] [524871].pdf To learn more about this opportunity, please contact Professor Vince Wallace at vincent.wallace@uwa.edu.au For enquiries on the recruitment process, please contact Talent Advisor at petrina.gomez@uwa.edu.au How to apply Please apply online via the Apply Now button and address the selection criteria in your submission in a separate document. For information to assist you with compiling statements to answer the selection criteria, please visit Addressing the selection criteria . You may submit a statement describing any personal circumstances relevant to your application. This can include academic/professional interruptions or achievements relative to opportunity. Closing date: 11:55 PM AWST on Tuesday, 15 December 2026. Interviews will be held in mid to late January 2027. This position is open to international applicants. Benefits Flexible working options. Generous paid leave including parental leave. Up to 17% employer paid superannuation. Salary packaging available. Incremental progression based on 12 months continuous service. Up to 25% discount on eligible UWA full-fee courses. Discounted health insurance. Employee Assistance Program. About the University The University of Western Australia (UWA) is ranked among the top 100 universities in the world and a member of the prestigious Australian Group of Eight research intensive universities. With a strong research track record, vibrant campus and working environments, supported by the freedom to innovate and inspire, there is no better time to join Western Australia’s top university. Learn more about us. Our commitment to inclusion and diversity UWA is committed to a diverse workforce and an equitable and inclusive workplace. We are committed to fostering a safe environment for all, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, women, those from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, the LGBTIQA+ community, and people living with disability. If you require any reasonable adjustments, we encourage you to advise us at the time of application. Alternatively, you can contact us for assistance during the recruitment process. If you have queries relating to your application, please contact the individual named in the advertisement. To enable a quick response, please include the 6-digit job reference number. Back to job search Other jobs School Operations Officer Crawley, WA Student Administration / Support Services Join a collaborative team delivering high-quality operational and administrative support to academics, researchers and students. Senior Lecturer - Medical School – Year 2 Students Crawley, WA Health and Medical Sciences Deliver high-impact clinical skills education within UWA's Doctor of Medicine (MD) program, focusing on patient assessment, clinical reasoning, and examination skills. Senior Lecturer - Medical School – Year 1 Clinical Skills Crawley, WA Health and Medical Sciences Play a key role in shaping the next generation of medical practitioners through high-quality clinical skills education. Benefits Flexible working options considered. 26 weeks paid parental leave after one year and 36 weeks after five years continuous service, regardless of gender. 4 weeks annual leave with the option to purchase more. 13 weeks long service leave after seven/ten years. Salary packaging options. 17% superannuation, with the option to reduce to the minimum super guarantee. Up to 25% discount on eligible UWA full-fee courses. Discounted health insurance, and convenient on-campus childcare options. Incremental progression based on 12 months continuous service. Excluding casual positions. The University of Western Australia acknowledges that its campus is situated on Noongar land, and that Noongar people remain the spiritual and cultural custodians of their land, and continue to practise their values, languages, beliefs and knowledge. Indigenous commitment Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X Youtube Cookie Preferences Privacy Policy © 2026 The University of Western Australia Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
