    "https://external.jobs.uwa.edu.au/jobs/senior-lecturer-associate-professor-uwa-law-school-s50d-crawley-wa-australia": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer / Associate Professor - UWA Law School s50d",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer / Associate Professor - UWA Law School s50d - Crawley, WA, Australia Back to UWA My Profile Kiosk mode Pause the proceeding carousel Senior Lecturer / Associate Professor - UWA Law School s50d 525013 Crawley, WA Full Time Closing on: 30 Nov 2026 Teaching and research position. Play a role in fostering best practice teaching, research and engagement related to Indigenous peoples. Full-time appointment on a tenurable basis. Base salary range: Level C, $149,909 – $172,441 p.a. (pro-rata) plus 17% superannuation, or Level D, $179,945 – $197,969 p.a. (pro-rata) plus 17% superannuation. About the area Established in 1927, UWA Law School is the fifth oldest Law School in Australia and is Western Australia’s premier Law School.  We have a long and proud history of academic excellence, providing a broad range of education programs and leading research projects with impact. We are committed to contributing to our local, national and international communities with the continuing valued support of our alumni and national and international partners. The Law School comprises over fifty scholars engaged in teaching and research in law, socio-legal studies and criminology. Alongside our qualifying law degree – the post-graduate Juris Doctor (JD), the Law School offers three undergraduate majors ( Business Law , Law and Society , and Criminology ), a Bachelor of Criminology and Criminal Justice, and several Masters courses.  Continually evolving to meet current and future challenges, we have a particular focus on the Indigenisation of the curriculum, internationalisation and multi-disciplinarity. Our academic staff have been drawn from top universities around the world and have broad-ranging expertise in delivering quality teaching programs. Scholars within the Law School are engaged in a diverse range of legal and socio-legal research areas covering public, private, international and domestic law, as well as socio legal and justice studies, and criminology. Our scholars attract competitive research funding and play leading roles in law reform and policy development, both domestically and internationally. Our research projects are focused on producing valuable scholarship with real-world impact through a variety of written outputs (books, journal articles, research reports, and submissions) as well as disseminating information through public lectures, workshops, online and within our taught programs. About the opportunity Contribute to the Law School's teaching programs, research, and engagement. Play a role in fostering best practice teaching, research and engagement related to Indigenous peoples. About you Completion of a professional Law degree (Bachelor of Laws or Juris Doctor) together with a PhD or progress towards completion of PhD; or completion of other higher degree qualifications; or significant industry or professional experience. Demonstrated track record in the development and delivery of tertiary curricula; or demonstrated track record of leading the development and delivery of initiatives to educate the profession and other stakeholders regarding legal issues affecting Indigenous peoples and in the development of innovative approaches to engage Indigenous peoples with the law. Track record of research evidenced by peer-reviewed journal articles and other publications with research impact; or track record of delivering programs and other initiatives related to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples in legal contexts. Experience leading activities that foster Indigenous engagement in legal contexts and promote understanding of Indigenous legal issues in post-secondary institutions and/or government or industry. Note : As part of the recruitment process, candidates will be required to complete declarations in accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence. This position is only available for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Island people as permitted under Section 50(d) of the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 . UWA believes that an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander individual would bring essential insights and understanding to this role, enhancing the university’s ability to connect with and serve the community effectively. Position descriptions: PD - Senior Lecturer (525013).pdf and PD - Associate Professor (525013).pdf To learn more about this opportunity, please contact Professor Sharon Mascher at dean-law@uwa.edu.au How to apply Please apply online via the Apply Now button and address the selection criteria in your submission in a separate document. For information to assist you with compiling statements to answer the selection criteria, please visit Addressing the selection criteria . You may submit a statement describing any personal circumstances relevant to your application. This can include academic/professional interruptions or achievements relative to opportunity. Closing date: 11:55 PM AWST on Monday, 30 November 2026. This position is only open to applicants with relevant rights to work in Australia. Benefits Flexible working options. Generous paid leave including parental leave. Up to 17% employer paid superannuation. Salary packaging available. Incremental progression based on 12 months continuous service. Up to 25% discount on eligible UWA full-fee courses. Discounted health insurance. Employee Assistance Program. About the University The University of Western Australia (UWA) is ranked among the top 100 universities in the world and a member of the prestigious Australian Group of Eight research intensive universities. With a strong research track record, vibrant campus and working environments, supported by the freedom to innovate and inspire, there is no better time to join Western Australia’s top university. Learn more about us. Our commitment to inclusion and diversity UWA is committed to a diverse workforce and an equitable and inclusive workplace. We are committed to fostering a safe environment for all, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, women, those from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, the LGBTIQA+ community, and people living with disability. If you require any reasonable adjustments, we encourage you to advise us at the time of application. Alternatively, you can contact us for assistance during the recruitment process. If you have queries relating to your application, please contact the individual named in the advertisement. Queries regarding indigenous aspects of the role may be directed to indigenous.workforce@uwa.edu.au To enable a quick response, please include the 6-digit job reference number. Back to job search Apply Now First Name (required) ded19feb Last Name (required) d936fe7d Email (required) fb3032d6 Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Other jobs School Operations Officer Crawley, WA Student Administration / Support Services Join a collaborative team delivering high-quality operational and administrative support to academics, researchers and students. Senior Lecturer - Medical School – Year 2 Students Crawley, WA Health and Medical Sciences Deliver high-impact clinical skills education within UWA's Doctor of Medicine (MD) program, focusing on patient assessment, clinical reasoning, and examination skills. Senior Lecturer - Medical School – Year 1 Clinical Skills Crawley, WA Health and Medical Sciences Play a key role in shaping the next generation of medical practitioners through high-quality clinical skills education. Benefits Flexible working options considered. 26 weeks paid parental leave after one year and 36 weeks after five years continuous service, regardless of gender. 4 weeks annual leave with the option to purchase more. 13 weeks long service leave after seven/ten years. Salary packaging options. 17% superannuation, with the option to reduce to the minimum super guarantee. Up to 25% discount on eligible UWA full-fee courses. Discounted health insurance, and convenient on-campus childcare options. Incremental progression based on 12 months continuous service. Excluding casual positions. The University of Western Australia acknowledges that its campus is situated on Noongar land, and that Noongar people remain the spiritual and cultural custodians of their land, and continue to practise their values, languages, beliefs and knowledge. Indigenous commitment Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X Youtube Cookie Preferences Privacy Policy © 2026 The University of Western Australia Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
    "https://external.jobs.uwa.edu.au/jobs/publications-officer-crawley-wa-australia": {
      "title": "Publications Officer",
      "description": "Publications Officer - Crawley, WA, Australia Back to UWA My Profile Kiosk mode Pause the proceeding carousel Publications Officer 524992 Crawley, WA Full Time Closing on: 7 Dec 2026 Join a respected mining research and education centre that improves safety and productivity across the global mining industry. Use your copyediting and desktop publishing expertise to produce high-quality technical and academic publications with real-world impact. Full-time (1 FTE) appointment on an ongoing/fixed term basis for 8 months. Base salary range: $86,540 – $95,920 p.a. (pro-rata) plus 17% superannuation. About the area The Australian Centre for Geomechanics (ACG) is a multidisciplinary research centre within The University of Western Australia and a recognised leader in mining geomechanics. Working closely with industry, researchers and technical experts worldwide, the ACG delivers applied research, professional education, training programs, conferences and publications that contribute to safer and more productive mining operations globally. A key part of the ACG's mission is sharing research outcomes and industry knowledge through high-quality publications, conference proceedings and learning resources. About the opportunity Copyedit, proofread, format and publish conference proceedings, technical reports, training materials and promotional publications to a professional standard. Coordinate the end-to-end publication production process, including managing technical reviews and liaising with authors, editors and reviewers. Support the publication of conference proceedings to the ACG's online repository while maintaining publication records, templates and style guides. About you Tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant work experience in copyediting and desktop publishing, or equivalent relevant experience/competency. Strong experience using Microsoft Word, including styles, templates and track changes, with the ability to prepare publication-ready documents. Demonstrated experience editing academic, technical or research-based content and applying recognised style guides with a high level of accuracy. Excellent written communication, organisation and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to manage multiple deadlines and priorities. Exceptional attention to detail, strong research capability and a commitment to delivering high-quality publications. Experience with citation formatting and Adobe InDesign will be highly regarded. Note : As part of the recruitment process, candidates will be required to complete declarations in accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence. Position description: PD - Publications Officer (524992).pdf To learn more about this opportunity, please contact Garth Doig at Garth.Doig@uwa.edu.au For enquiries on the recruitment process, please contact Talent Advisor at Ritika.Dhawan@uwa.edu.au How to apply Please apply online via the Apply Now button. The content of your Resume and Cover Letter should demonstrate how you meet the selection criteria. Closing date: 11:55 PM AWST on Monday 7th December 2026. This position is only open to applicants with relevant rights to work in Australia. Please note: Unsolicited applications from recruitment agencies will not be accepted outside of formal channels. Benefits Flexible working options. Generous paid leave including parental leave. Up to 17% employer paid superannuation. Salary packaging available. Incremental progression based on 12 months continuous service. Up to 25% discount on eligible UWA full-fee courses. Discounted health insurance. Employee Assistance Program. About the University The University of Western Australia (UWA) is ranked among the top 100 universities in the world and a member of the prestigious Australian Group of Eight research intensive universities. With a strong research track record, vibrant campus and working environments, supported by the freedom to innovate and inspire, there is no better time to join Western Australia’s top university. Learn more about us. Our commitment to inclusion and diversity UWA is committed to a diverse workforce and an equitable and inclusive workplace. We are committed to fostering a safe environment for all, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, women, those from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, the LGBTIQA+ community, and people living with disability. If you require any reasonable adjustments, we encourage you to advise us at the time of application. Alternatively, you can contact us for assistance during the recruitment process. If you have queries relating to your application, please contact the individual named in the advertisement. To enable a quick response, please include the 6-digit job reference number. Back to job search Other jobs Student Support Officer Claremont, WA Student Administration / Support Services Build strong relationships with students, University stakeholders and external service providers to ensure students receive timely, culturally responsive support and access to the services they need to succeed Lecturer - Computer Science Crawley, WA Engineering and Mathematical Sciences Shape the future of computing through high-quality teaching, research, and student supervision. Lecturer - Computer Science and Software Engineering Crawley, WA Engineering and Mathematical Sciences Advance teaching, research and collaboration in computer science and software engineering within a leading academic department. Benefits Flexible working options considered. 26 weeks paid parental leave after one year and 36 weeks after five years continuous service, regardless of gender. 4 weeks annual leave with the option to purchase more. 13 weeks long service leave after seven/ten years. Salary packaging options. 17% superannuation, with the option to reduce to the minimum super guarantee. Up to 25% discount on eligible UWA full-fee courses. Discounted health insurance, and convenient on-campus childcare options. Incremental progression based on 12 months continuous service. Excluding casual positions. The University of Western Australia acknowledges that its campus is situated on Noongar land, and that Noongar people remain the spiritual and cultural custodians of their land, and continue to practise their values, languages, beliefs and knowledge. Indigenous commitment Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X Youtube Cookie Preferences Privacy Policy © 2026 The University of Western Australia Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
    "https://external.jobs.uwa.edu.au/jobs/student-support-officer-claremont-wa-australia": {
      "title": "Student Support Officer",
      "description": "Student Support Officer - Claremont, WA, Australia Back to UWA My Profile Kiosk mode Pause the proceeding carousel Student Support Officer 525060 Claremont, WA Part Time Closing on: 30 Nov 2026 Make a meaningful difference in the lives of international students by supporting their wellbeing, success and transition to study in Australia. Join UWA's Centre for English Language Teaching (CELT), a globally focused team delivering high-quality English language programs and student support services. Part-time (0.5866 FTE - 44 hours per fortnight) appointment on a fixed term basis for 12 months working Monday to Thursday, 8:00am to 2:00pm. Base salary range: $86,540 – $95,920 p.a. (pro-rata) plus 17% superannuation. About the area The Centre for English Language Teaching (CELT) is a key part of the Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education and Student Experience), delivering high-quality English language programs and services to international students. CELT supports the University's international strategy by helping students develop English language skills, successfully transition to further study, and thrive in their academic and personal lives. The team works collaboratively to create a safe, inclusive and supportive environment for students from diverse cultural and linguistic backgrounds. About the opportunity Provide wellbeing, welfare and study support to international students, delivering advice, guidance and referrals to help students navigate personal, social, housing and academic challenges. Coordinate student support initiatives including orientation activities, safety and wellbeing programs, critical incident support, and guidance on further study pathways within Australia. Build strong relationships with students, University stakeholders and external service providers to ensure students receive timely, culturally responsive support and access to the services they need to succeed About you Relevant tertiary qualification or equivalent experience in student services, student welfare, case coordination or a related field. Demonstrated experience supporting international students or culturally diverse communities, including identifying needs, providing advice and facilitating referrals to appropriate services. Excellent communication and relationship-management skills, with the ability to explain complex information, facilitate presentations or orientation sessions, and engage effectively with a wide range of stakeholders. Sound judgement and the ability to support students experiencing wellbeing, welfare, health or safety concerns while maintaining confidentiality and managing sensitive matters appropriately. Strong organisational, administrative and digital literacy skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities, maintain accurate records and work both independently and collaboratively. Note : As part of the recruitment process, candidates will be required to complete declarations in accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence. A current Working with Children Check & a National Police clearance will be required for the successful applicant. Flexible working days may be required. Position description: PD - Student Support Officer (525060).pdf To learn more about this opportunity, please contact Shiau Lin Seah Barnett at shiaulin.barnett@uwa.edu.au For enquiries on the recruitment process, please contact Ritika Dhawan at ritika.dhawan@uwa.edu.au How to apply Please apply online via the Apply Now button. The content of your Resume and Cover Letter should demonstrate how you meet the selection criteria. Closing date: 11:55 PM AWST on Monday 30th November 2026 This position is only open to applicants with relevant rights to work in Australia Please note: Unsolicited applications from recruitment agencies will not be accepted outside of formal channels. Benefits Generous paid leave including parental leave. Up to 17% employer paid superannuation. Salary packaging available. Incremental progression based on 12 months continuous service. Up to 25% discount on eligible UWA full-fee courses. Discounted health insurance. Employee Assistance Program. About the University The University of Western Australia (UWA) is ranked among the top 100 universities in the world and a member of the prestigious Australian Group of Eight research intensive universities. With a strong research track record, vibrant campus and working environments, supported by the freedom to innovate and inspire, there is no better time to join Western Australia’s top university. Learn more about us. Our commitment to inclusion and diversity UWA is committed to a diverse workforce and an equitable and inclusive workplace. We are committed to fostering a safe environment for all, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, women, those from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, the LGBTIQA+ community, and people living with disability. If you require any reasonable adjustments, we encourage you to advise us at the time of application. Alternatively, you can contact us for assistance during the recruitment process. If you have queries relating to your application, please contact the individual named in the advertisement. To enable a quick response, please include the 6-digit job reference number. Back to job search Other jobs Publications Officer Crawley, WA Administration & Executive support, Community Engagement/Marketing, Research support Use your copyediting and publishing skills to support leading mining research, education and industry knowledge sharing. Lecturer - Computer Science Crawley, WA Engineering and Mathematical Sciences Shape the future of computing through high-quality teaching, research, and student supervision. Lecturer - Computer Science and Software Engineering Crawley, WA Engineering and Mathematical Sciences Advance teaching, research and collaboration in computer science and software engineering within a leading academic department. Benefits Flexible working options considered. 26 weeks paid parental leave after one year and 36 weeks after five years continuous service, regardless of gender. 4 weeks annual leave with the option to purchase more. 13 weeks long service leave after seven/ten years. Salary packaging options. 17% superannuation, with the option to reduce to the minimum super guarantee. Up to 25% discount on eligible UWA full-fee courses. Discounted health insurance, and convenient on-campus childcare options. Incremental progression based on 12 months continuous service. Excluding casual positions. The University of Western Australia acknowledges that its campus is situated on Noongar land, and that Noongar people remain the spiritual and cultural custodians of their land, and continue to practise their values, languages, beliefs and knowledge. Indigenous commitment Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X Youtube Cookie Preferences Privacy Policy © 2026 The University of Western Australia Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
